The reasoning provided is **correct and complete**. The matrix $\mathbf{P}$ that projects any vector $\mathbf{v} = \begin{pmatrix} x \\ y \\ z \end{pmatrix}$ onto the $yz$-plane is:

$$
\mathbf{P} = \begin{pmatrix}
0 & 0 & 0 \\
0 & 1 & 0 \\
0 & 0 & 1
\end{pmatrix}
$$

This matrix sets the $x$-component of the vector to zero while preserving the $y$ and $z$ components, which is exactly what projection onto the $yz$-plane entails.

